      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m interpreting this as we end up with the following mapping (gpio to pin):\n\n```\n318 -\u003e 30\n319 -\u003e 31\n320 -\u003e 32\n321 -\u003e 33\n322 -\u003e 34\n323 -\u003e 35\n```\n\nAm I interpreting that correctly?\n\nIf so, rather than trying to sort and do various range calculations, etc., can you just create a map directly from the above information?\n\n```\ngpio_to_pin_map[318] \u003d 30\n...\n```\n\nI guess that may not be enough since a given device (`INT1055:00` vs `INT1055:01`) will potentially have overlapping pins?\n\nIn that case maybe you could store as:\n\n```\ngpio_to_pin_map[318] \u003d {\u0027pin\u0027: 30, \u0027device\u0027: \u0027INTC1055:01\u0027}\n```\n\nWould that give you the ability to look up the information you need? \n\nI see that in a later commit you are getting some information like this:\n\n```\n       #  detect \u003d {\n        #       \u0027INTC1055:00\u0027: \u0027gpiochip0\u0027,\n        #       \u0027INTC1055:01\u0027: \u0027gpiochip1\u0027,\n        #       \u0027INTC1055:02\u0027: \u0027gpiochip2\u0027\n        #  }\n        #\n        # bases \u003d {\n        #       \u0027gpiochip0\u0027: 344\n        #       \u0027gpiochip1\u0027: 288\n        #       \u0027gpiochip2\u0027: 152\n        # }\n```\n\nbut I haven\u0027t followed all the code through to see what direction you are trying to map things to/from. Ultimately, are you trying to go from a GPIO to a `pin` on a `device` or something different (perhaps reverse)?",
